---
import BaseLayout from '~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import '~/styles/prose.css'
import { SITE } from '~/config/site'
---
<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="About Wright Industries — who, what, why."
  documentStripLeft="DOCUMENT · ABOUT"
  largeWordmark={false}
>
  <article class="container about">
    <header class="about-header">
      <p class="kicker mono-chrome">ABOUT</p>
      <h1 class="about-title">{SITE.title}</h1>
      <p class="about-lede">{SITE.tagline}</p>
    </header>

    <hr class="rule" />

    <div class="prose about-body">
      <p>
        This site is a working notebook. It documents the design, manufacture,
        and refurbishment of nixie tubes and the clocks and electronics built
        around them — published in public as the work happens rather than
        waiting for finished results.
      </p>

      <h2>Who</h2>
      <p>
        Wright Industries is the working name for the workshop of Joel Wright
        in Brisbane, Australia. The sole-trader business
        <em>J WRIGHT INDUSTRIES</em> (ABN 15 724 733 509) is registered to
        ship small runs of custom-built nixie clocks, tube refurbishments, and
        related electronics. Phase one is the documentation site you're
        reading.
      </p>

      <h2>What</h2>
      <p>
        Each <strong>project</strong> documents one body of work — a clock, a
        driver board, a piece of workshop tooling — and contains two kinds of
        content:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Notes</strong> are rough, dated build-log entries. Questions
          asked of myself, experiments tried, dead ends hit, decisions
          recorded with their reasoning. Most of the volume here is notes.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Releases</strong> are longer, more refined milestone
          write-ups intended to stand on their own. Schematic releases,
          procedure documents, retrospectives, the occasional manifesto.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Why</h2>
      <p>
        Most of the nixie literature lives in scattered forum threads and
        out-of-print Soviet manuals. The intent here is to add to the public
        record in a way that is durable, searchable, and honest about the
        messy in-between work — not just polished outcomes.
      </p>

      <p>
        If something here is useful to you, that is the point. If something
        here is wrong, please tell me — there's a <a href="/contact">contact
        form</a> and an RSS feed. If it's useful enough that you'd like to
        shout the workshop a coffee, there's a <a
        href="https://buymeacoffee.com/wrightindustries" rel="noopener
        noreferrer" target="_blank">tip jar</a> — entirely optional, never
        expected.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about { padding-block: var(--s-6) var(--s-8); }
  .about-header { max-width: var(--measure); }
  .kicker { color: var(--c-ink-muted); margin-bottom: var(--s-2); }
  .about-title {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--t-d1);
    line-height: var(--t-d1-lh);
    font-weight: var(--w-display-light);
    font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 60;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  }
  .about-lede {
    margin-top: var(--s-3);
    font-size: var(--t-body-lg);
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--c-ink-muted);
    font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 24;
  }
  .rule {
    margin-block: var(--s-5);
    background: var(--c-rule);
  }
  .about-body { max-width: var(--measure); }
</style>
